  </header>

  <section id="executive-overview">
    <h2>Executive Overview</h2>
    <p class="lede">The dataset shows a long transition from very low, administered-price conditions in the early 1970s to a high-amplitude, globally traded commodity regime. The first monthly observation is {fmt_price(first['price'])} in {fmt_d(first['date'])}; the final observation is {fmt_price(last['price'])} in {fmt_d(last['date'])}. Over that {years:.2f}-year interval, the nominal price CAGR is <strong>{cagr*100:.2f}%</strong>.</p>
    <div class="grid kpi-grid">
      {''.join(f'<div class="card"><div class="kpi-label">{esc(k)}</div><div class="kpi-value">{esc(v)}</div><div class="kpi-note">{esc(n)}</div></div>' for k,v,n in kpis)}
    </div>
    <div class="callout"><strong>Key read-through:</strong> the long-run average is {fmt_price(statistics.mean(prices))}, but the median is materially lower at {fmt_price(statistics.median(prices))}, indicating a right-skewed distribution shaped by the 2008 peak, the 2011–2014 high-price plateau, and the 2022 spike.</div>
  </section>

  <section id="long-term-trend">
    <h2>Long-Term Price Trajectory (1970–2026)</h2>
    <p>The full time series is the backbone of the analysis. It identifies four broad regimes: a low-price pre-shock baseline through the early 1970s; a step-change higher in the 1970s and early 1980s; a lower and comparatively range-bound 1986–1999 period; and a post-2000 high-volatility era with repeated boom-bust episodes.</p>
    <div class="chart-wrap">{line_chart(rows)}</div>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Scale shift:</strong> prices rose from {fmt_price(first['price'])} in {fmt_d(first['date'])} to {fmt_price(point(date(1974,1,1))['price'])} in Jan 1974, a {point(date(1974,1,1))['mom_pct']:+.1f}% month-over-month jump and {point(date(1974,1,1))['yoy_pct']:+.1f}% year-over-year increase.</li>
      <li><strong>Peak:</strong> the maximum monthly price is {fmt_price(max_row['price'])} in {fmt_d(max_row['date'])}, immediately before the sharp 2008 financial-crisis-era contraction visible in the series.</li>
      <li><strong>Latest reading:</strong> the final point, {fmt_price(last['price'])} in {fmt_d(last['date'])}, is below the 2008 all-time high by {fmt_price(max_row['price']-last['price'])}, but above the full-sample mean by {fmt_price(last['price']-statistics.mean(prices))}.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="decade-by-decade">
