      <li><strong>2000s–2010s:</strong> the 2000s averaged {fmt_price(decades[3]['mean'])} with sample volatility of {fmt_price(decades[3]['std'])}; the 2010s averaged {fmt_price(decades[4]['mean'])} despite ending {fmt_pct(decades[4]['change_pct'])} lower than Jan 2010.</li>
      <li><strong>2020s:</strong> through {fmt_d(last['date'])}, the decade average is {fmt_price(decades[5]['mean'])}; interpretation should account for the partial 2026 coverage and the source note that some late-period values may be estimated or projected.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="volatility-and-shocks">
    <h2>Price Volatility &amp; Major Shocks</h2>
    <p>Volatility is episodic rather than evenly distributed. The largest absolute monthly move is {max_abs_mom['mom_abs']:+.2f} USD/bbl in {fmt_d(max_abs_mom['date'])}; the largest positive monthly percentage move is {max_pos_mom_pct['mom_pct']:+.1f}% in {fmt_d(max_pos_mom_pct['date'])}; and the largest negative monthly percentage move is {max_neg_mom_pct['mom_pct']:+.1f}% in {fmt_d(max_neg_mom_pct['date'])}.</p>
    <div class="chart-wrap">{shock_chart(shock_data)}</div>
