    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrap">{annual_heat(annual)}</div>
    <p class="note">The annual heat strip uses mean monthly price by calendar year. The highest annual mean is {fmt_price(highest_annual['mean'])} in {highest_annual['year']}; the lowest is {fmt_price(lowest_annual['mean'])} in {lowest_annual['year']}. The most volatile calendar year by sample standard deviation is {most_volatile_year['year']} at {fmt_price(most_volatile_year['std'])}.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="outlook-and-conclusions">
    <h2>Outlook &amp; Conclusions</h2>
    <p>The 2020s portion of the dataset presents a sequence of compression, rebound, spike, and moderation: annual average price moves from {fmt_price(annual_by_year[2020]['mean'])} in 2020 to {fmt_price(annual_by_year[2022]['mean'])} in 2022, then eases to {fmt_price(annual_by_year[2025]['mean'])} in 2025. The partial 2026 average is {fmt_price(annual_by_year[2026]['mean'])}, based on {annual_by_year[2026]['months']} monthly observations through {fmt_d(last['date'])}.</p>
