69}}.bar-label{{font-size:12px;fill:#0f172a;font-weight:700}}.tiny-label{{font-size:10px;fill:#334155;font-weight:700}}.small{{font-size:10px}}table{{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:14px;font-size:14px}}th,td{{border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);padding:10px 9px;text-align:right;vertical-align:top}}th:first-child,td:first-child{{text-align:left}}th{{color:#334155;font-size:12px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.06em;background:#f8fafc}}.two-col{{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.15fr .85fr;gap:18px;align-items:start}}.callout{{border-left:4px solid var(--blue);background:#f1f7fc;padding:14px 16px;border-radius:10px;color:#1e3a5f;margin:14px 0}}.note{{color:var(--muted);font-size:13px}}ul{{margin:10px 0 0 22px;padding:0}}li{{margin:7px 0}}.footer{{color:#64748b;font-size:12px;text-align:center;margin-top:22px}}@media(max-width:900px){{.kpi-grid,.two-col{{grid-template-columns:1fr}}.hero{{padding:30px}}h1{{font-size:32px}}section{{padding:20px}}}}@media print{{body{{background:white}}.container{{max-width:none;padding:0}}section,.hero{{box-shadow:none;break-inside:avoid}}.chart{{min-width:0}}}}
</style></head><body><main class="container">
<header class="hero"><h1>Global Crude Oil Price Trends: A Historical Analysis (1970–2026)</h1><p class="subtitle">A data-forward review of 675 monthly nominal crude oil price observations, highlighting long-run regimes, decade-level shifts, volatility clusters, and major price shocks.</p><div class="meta"><span class="pill">Source file: /app/benchmark/data.csv</span><span class="pill">Monthly frequency</span><span class="pill">USD per barrel</span><span class="pill">Span: {fmt_d(first['date'])}–{fmt_d(last['date'])}</span></div></header>
<section id="executive-overview"><h2>Executive Overview</h2><p class="lede">The dataset shows a long transition from very low, administered-price conditions in the early 1970s to a high-amplitude, globally traded commodity regime. The first monthly observation is {fmt_price(first['price'])} in {fmt_d(first['date'])}; the final observation is {fmt_price(last['price'])} in {fmt_d(last['date'])}. Over that {years:.2f}-year interval, the nominal price CAGR is <strong>{cagr*100:.2f}%</strong>.</p><div class="grid kpi-grid">{''.join(f'<div class="card"><div class="kpi-label">{esc(k)}</div><div class="kpi-value">{esc(v)}</div><div class="kpi-note">{esc(n)}</div></div>' for k,v,n in kpis)}</div><div class="callout"><strong>Key read-through:</strong> the long-run average is {fmt_price(statistics.mean(prices))}, but the median is materially lower at {fmt_price(statistics.median(prices))}, indicating a right-skewed distribution shaped by the 2008 peak, the 2011–2014 high-price plateau, and the 2022 spike.</div></section>
<section id="long-term-trend"><h2>Long-Term Price Trajectory (1970–2026)</h2><p>The full time series is the backbone of the analysis. It identifies four broad regimes: a low-price pre-shock baseline through the early 1970s; a step-change higher in the 1970s and early 1980s; a lower and comparatively range-bound 1986–1999 period; and a post-2000 high-volatility era with repeated boom-bust episodes.</p><div class="chart-wrap">{line_chart(rows)}</div><ul><li><strong>Scale shift:</strong> prices rose from {fmt_price(first['price'])} in {fmt_d(first['date'])} to {fmt_price(point(date(1974,1,1))['price'])} in Jan 1974, a {point(date(1974,1,1))['mom_pct']:+.1f}% month-over-month jump and {point(date(1974,1,1))['yoy_pct']:+.1f}% year-over-year increase.</li><li><strong>Peak:</strong> the maximum monthly price is {fmt_price(max_row['price'])} in {fmt_d(max_row['date'])}, immediately before the sharp 2008 financial-crisis-era contraction visible in the series.</li><li><strong>Latest reading:</strong> the final point, {fmt_price(last['price'])} in {fmt_d(last['date'])}, is below the 2008 all-time high by {fmt_price(max_row['price']-last['price'])}, but above the full-sample mean by {fmt_price(last['price']-statistics.mean(prices))}.</li></ul></section>
<section id="decade-by-decade"><h2>Decade-by-Decade Breakdown</h2><p>Decade comparisons clarify the structural change in pricing regimes. The 2010s have the highest decade-average monthly price at {fmt_price(highest_dec['mean'])}, while the 2000s show the widest intra-decade range because they include both the early-2000s build-up and the 2008 peak-to-collapse cycle.</p><div class="chart-wrap">{decade_chart(decades)}</div><table><thead><tr><th>Decade</th><th>Months</th><th>Mean</th><th>Median</th><th>Std. dev.</th><th>Min</th><th>Max</th><th>Range</th><th>First-to-last</th></tr></thead><tbody>{decade_table}</tbody></table><h3>Regime interpretation</h3><ul><li><strong>1970s:</strong> mean price {fmt_price(decades[0]['mean'])}; first-to-last increase {fmt_pct(decades[0]['change_pct'])}, dominated by the 1974 step-change and late-1979 spike.</li><li><strong>1980s–1990s:</strong> the 1980s averaged {fmt_price(decades[1]['mean'])} but ended {fmt_pct(decades[1]['change_pct'])} from Jan 1980 to Dec 1989; the 1990s averaged a lower {fmt_price(decades[2]['mean'])} with the lowest decade volatility in the table ({fmt_price(decades[2]['std'])}).</li><li><strong>2000s–2010s:</strong> the 2000s averaged {fmt_price(decades[3]['mean'])} with sample volatility of {fmt_price(decades[3]['std'])}; the 2010s averaged {fmt_price(decades[4]['mean'])} despite ending {fmt_pct(decades[4]['change_pct'])} lower than Jan 2010.</li><li><strong>2020s:</strong> through {fmt_d(last['date'])}, the decade average is {fmt_price(decades[5]['mean'])}; interpretation should account for the partial 2026 coverage and the source note that some late-period values may be estimated or projected.</li></ul></section>
<section id="volatility-and-shocks"><h2>Price Volatility &amp; Major Shocks</h2><p>Volatility is episodic rather than evenly distributed. The largest absolute monthly move is {max_abs_mom['mom_abs']:+.2f} USD/bbl in {fmt_d(max_abs_mom['date'])}; the largest positive monthly percentage move is {max_pos_mom_pct['mom_pct']:+.1f}% in {fmt_d(max_pos_mom_pct['date'])}; and the largest negative monthly percentage move is {max_neg_mom_pct['mom_pct']:+.1f}% in {fmt_d(max_neg_mom_pct['date'])}.</p><div class="chart-wrap">{shock_chart(shock_data)}</div><div class="two-col"><div><h3>Largest absolute month-over-month shocks</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Month</th><th>Price</th><th>MoM USD</th><th>MoM %</th><th>YoY USD</th><th>YoY %</th></tr></thead><tbody>{shock_table}</tbody></table></div><div><h3>Largest absolute year-over-year shocks</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Month</th><th>Price</th><th>YoY USD</th><th>YoY %</th></tr></thead><tbody>{yoy_table}</tbody></table></div></div><ul><li><strong>1974 oil shock in the data:</strong> Jan 1974 records {fmt_price(point(date(1974,1,1))['price'])}, up {point(date(1974,1,1))['mom_abs']:+.2f} USD/bbl month over month and {point(date(1974,1,1))['yoy_pct']:+.1f}% year over year.</li><li><strong>2008 crisis cycle:</strong> Jul 2008 is the dataset maximum at {fmt_price(max_row['price'])}; Oct 2008 has the largest absolute MoM drop ({max_abs_mom['mom_abs']:+.2f} USD/bbl), and Jul 2009 has the largest absolute YoY drop ({max_abs_yoy['yoy_abs']:+.2f} USD/bbl).</li><li><strong>COVID-period shock:</strong> Mar 2020 falls to {fmt_price(point(date(2020,3,1))['price'])}, a {point(date(2020,3,1))['mom_pct']:+.1f}% MoM change, the largest negative monthly percentage change in the dataset.</li><li><strong>2022 repricing:</strong> Mar 2022 reaches {fmt_price(point(date(2022,3,1))['price'])}, up {point(date(2022,3,1))['mom_abs']:+.2f} USD/bbl and {point(date(2022,3,1))['mom_pct']:+.1f}% versus Feb 2022.</li></ul></section>
<section id="statistical-summary"><h2>Statistical Summary &amp; Distribution</h2><p>The distribution is wide and right-skewed. The all-sample range is {fmt_price(max(prices)-min(prices))}; the interquartile band runs from {fmt_price(quantile(prices,.25))} to {fmt_price(quantile(prices,.75))}; and the 95th percentile is {fmt_price(quantile(prices,.95))}, reflecting the concentration of extreme high-price observations in the post-2000 period.</p><div class="two-col"><div class="chart-wrap">{histogram(prices)}</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Statistic</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody>{desc_table}</tbody></table></div></div><div class="chart-wrap">{annual_heat(annual)}</div><p class="note">The annual heat strip uses mean monthly price by calendar year. The highest annual mean is {fmt_price(highest_annual['mean'])} in {highest_annual['year']}; the lowest is {fmt_price(lowest_annual['mean'])} in {lowest_annual['year']}. The most volatile calendar year by sample standard deviation is {most_volatile_year['year']} at {fmt_price(most_volatile_year['std'])}.</p></section>
<section id="outlook-and-conclusions"><h2>Outlook &amp; Conclusions</h2><p>The 2020s portion of the dataset presents a sequence of compression, rebound, spike, and moderation: annual average price moves from {fmt_price(annual_by_year[2020]['mean'])} in 2020 to {fmt_price(annual_by_year[2022]['mean'])} in 2022, then eases to {fmt_price(annual_by_year[2025]['mean'])} in 2025. The partial 2026 average is {fmt_price(annual_by_year[2026]['mean'])}, based on {annual_by_year[2026]['months']} monthly observations through {fmt_d(last['date'])}.</p><ul><li><strong>Historical context:</strong> current and recent readings sit in a high nominal-price regime relative to the full-sample median of {fmt_price(statistics.median(prices))}, even though they remain below the 2008 maximum.</li><li><strong>Volatility baseline:</strong> analysts should benchmark short-term moves against the full-sample monthly standard deviation of {fmt_price(statistics.stdev(prices))} and the historical single-month extremes of {max_abs_mom['mom_abs']:+.2f} USD/bbl and {max_neg_mom_pct['mom_pct']:+.1f}%.</li><li><strong>Regime awareness:</strong> decade statistics show that price level and volatility are regime-dependent; the 1990s low-volatility average is a poor comparator for the 2000s–2020s environment.</li><li><strong>Data limitation:</strong> the source documentation notes that late-period observations approaching 2026 may be forecasts or estimates. This report does not fabricate or supplement values; all statistics are computed directly from the supplied CSV.</li></ul></section><div class="footer">Generated from /app/benchmark/data.csv. Calculations use nominal monthly prices; no inflation adjustment is applied.</div></main></body></html>'''
